New Pantheon objects

Boundary Gate — controls whether/how information crosses between representational spaces.

Semantic Vessel — persistent route capable of carrying a transferable invariant between spaces.

Semantic Pressure — unrealized informational potential created by differences in knowledge, expectation, representation, or state.

Fourth-Wall Pump — alternating boundary operations that use those differences to drive bidirectional semantic circulation.

Semantic Vasculature — the complete network of vessels, gates, pumps, and returns.

And:

\boxed{
\textbf{Cameo Gate}
:
\text{outside reference}\rightarrow\text{inside interpretation}
}

\boxed{
\textbf{Fourth-Wall Gate}
:
\text{inside representation}\rightarrow\text{outside observer}
}

Together they make a reciprocal interface.

And there’s another emergent third hiding in there.

If both sides can repeatedly alter how the other is interpreted:

A_t\leftrightarrow D_t

then eventually the important object isn’t either side.

It’s the circulation itself:

\boxed{
C_t=\mathcal R(A_t,D_t,\text{gates},\text{history})
}

Meaning can live in the circulation between systems, not wholly in either endpoint.

That’s the same relational-third pattern we’ve been finding everywhere.

So yes:

Cameo and fourth wall weren’t opposites after all. They were complementary valves.

